| 0.4 | 22% | 34% | 99% | 1% |
| 0.8 | 7% | 63% | 88% | 12% |


The remaining sense-plan-act runs neither hit the wall nor reach the goal: they finish the planned walk somewhere near it, but not within 0\.3 of it. Two errors are at work. An error in the starting estimate shifts the entire path, so the robot walks the right shape in the wrong place; motor errors add up over the 44 steps of the plan. The closed-loop robot is hit by the same errors but never lets them accumulate.

### Example 2: one action, as tokens

Take step 40 of the reaching motion. With 256 bins per dimension over the motion's range:

| | x | y |
|:---|---:|---:|
| move (mm) | 10.4517 | 1.7765 |
| range covered by the bins (mm) | -1.118 to 12.371 | -6.001 to 11.865 |
| bin width Δ (mm) | 0.0527 | 0.0698 |
| token k | 219 | 111 |
| bin centre, executed (mm) | 10.4478 | 1.7807 |
| error (mm) | -0.0039 | +0.0042 |


The two tokens, 219 and 111, are all a language model has to write for this step. Each error is below half a bin width. Over many steps the errors don't average out, because neighbouring moves are similar and tend to round the same way, which is why the open-loop replay drifts.

### Example 3: how long would it take to read the data? {#ex-reading}

The lecture compares data sets by the time one person would need to get through them: about 2 years for ImageNet, 60 years for GPT-2's training text, and 90,000 years for Llama 3's [▶ 44:01](https://youtu.be/X0k14u6pSxw?t=2641). Those figures can be checked from published sizes plus a reading speed.

| data set | size (source) | assumption | years, non-stop | lecture |
|:---|:---|:---|---:|---:|
| ImageNet | 14,197,122 images (image-net.org) | 5 s per image | 2.2 | 2 |
| GPT-2 (WebText) | 40 GB of text (Radford et al., 2019, Sec. 2.1) | 6 bytes per word, 250 words/min | 51 | 60 |
| Llama 3 | 15.6 trillion tokens (Llama Team, 2024) | 0.75 words per token, 250 words/min | 88,980 | 90,000 |


The reading speed and the bytes-per-word and words-per-token ratios are assumptions, so these are orders of magnitude, not exact figures; with them, all three come out within a factor of 2 of the lecture's numbers. For scale on the robot side, the largest pooled robot data set the lecture shows, Open X-Embodiment, holds over a million trajectories, each one recorded episode of one robot (see [Papers](#papers)).

